13.5

1. RGB to HSV spatial conversion

(1) RGB is designed from the principle of color light emission. Its color mixing method, such as when red, green, and blue light are
superimposed on each other, the colors are mixed, but the brightness is equal to the sum of the two brightness, the more mixed the
brightness, the higher is the additive mixing. The three channels of red, green and blue each have 256 levels of brightness, the darkest
at 0 and the brightest at 255.

(2) HSV is an intuitive color model, each of which is H (Hue), S (Saturation), and V (lightness Value).

• Hue (H) is measured by angle, and the range is 0°-360°.
• Saturation (S), the range is 0%-100%, the larger the value, the more saturated the color.
• The lightness (V) indicates the brightness of the color, and the range is 0%-100%.
The spatial domain diagram of HSV is shown in Fig. 14-1.

(3) The formula for converting RGB to HSV is shown in Fig. 14-2.

The Verilog implementation of RGB and HSV spatial domain conversion, the specific steps are as follows:

• According to the RGB to HSV formula, derive the formula of HSV to RGB by yourself.

• Use the existing RGB to HSV experimental program to perform timing simulation. The experimental code of RGB to HSV conversion
