2. Shape recognition algorithm

As the basis of image processing, shape recognition can be easily implemented using the cv library. But for SEA with limited resources,
the algorithm in the cv library cannot be realized if it is implemented in verilog.

Therefore, here we carry out simple shape recognition, such as circle, rectangle, triangle. For these three shapes, the conventional
idea is that we can perform feature recognition according to their geometric rules, such as corner points, there are four rectangles,
three triangles, and no circle (but you can take the mid-vertical line to find the intersection).

If we simplify the problem again and perform simple recognition, then some techniques can be used. For example, three rows of real-
time processing means that only three rows of data are stored each time, which effectively reduces the storage space. Then, we
observe the graph again, as shown in Fig. 14-3:

We can find out that:

• For a rectangle, the number of intersections on the three lines is not much different.
• For a triangle, the number of intersection points on the three lines satisfies: 1st line<2nd line <3rd line.
• For a circle, the number of intersections on the 2nd line always exceeds the 1st and 2nd lines.

We give the following algorithm:

(1) Perform grayscale processing on the input image, and then perform histogram equalization.
(2) Perform adaptive threshold binarization, and mark white 1 if it exceeds the threshold, otherwise mark black 0.
(3) Select three rows in the binarized image as rows to be processed.
(4) The intersection of each line and the graph (points in each line) is summed and counted, and mutation points are eliminated.
(5) Write Y1=∑X1, Y2=∑X2, Y3=∑X3.
(6) Compensate the difference, and then judge the shape.

