3. Color recognition and tracking
We have implemented the conversion for RGB to HSV and HSV to RGB. Then, for a specific color, we can determine the image by
setting a certain HSV range, as shown in Fig. 14-4, and the range of HSV corresponding to several colors is provided (H: 0-180; s: 0-
255; V: 0-255)

For non-specific colors, we need to make the camera read the HSV range of the color first, and then track the color. We provide
the following ideas:

(1) The method of pressing the button selects whether to perform color reading and tracking. The color reading needs to place a
fixed color object in a certain position in the camera image. This can be achieved through the HDMI display.

(2) For color tracking, it is impossible for us to search all the screens. This consumes a lot of resources and there are many
uncertain factors. Here we are based on the continuity of motion, the change between two adjacent frames cannot be very large.
Therefore, we can fine-tune the center range of the search point every time. For example, the red ball found in the previous frame
is at the (210,210) position, then the next time we search from the (210,210) position as the center. This can greatly reduce the
amount of calculation and avoid the problem of insufficient resources.
